Pat Rankin wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote innews:i7296p
> $kn2$1@news.m-online.net:
>> I was camping at the altar in the Caveman's Quest Home 1 level,
>> stacking some items for a polypile session and then I noticed...
>>   &    a major demon (hezrou called Tom) [seen: normal vision,
>>   infravision]
>> I don't recall to have ever seen a minor demon with a name, if it
>> wasn't been explicitly been given a name, either by me or by a former
>> player in case of a bones level. Has somebody an idea when such things
>> happen? (Are doppelgangers named, maybe?)
>
>      Ordinary monsters aren't given names when they're created.
> Hezrou and doggelganger are both ordinary.  This was probably
> someone's former pet.
>
> On Sep 18, 1:21 pm, APLer <AP...@floor.tilde> wrote:
>> No bones files? (I can't recall if the quest levels keep bones files or
>> not)
>
>      Some levels in the quest can have bones data, but not the first
> one.  The presence of the magic portal there inhibits bones on that
> level.  Ditto for the main dungeon level containing the other side
> of the portal.
>
>      My guess is that there was a bones level earlier in the dungeon,
> and while it was being explored, that former pet fell through a trap
> door or stepped on a level teleporter, either deliberately because
> it was badly wounded and the hero was nearby or accidentally while
> moving around.  (And for the wounded case, it might even have picked
> up and read a cursed scroll of teleportation.)  Then its random
> destination turned out to be the level with the quest entry portal.
> Once the current character eventually arrived there, the monster was
> able to move on that level, and while moving it could have wandered
> onto the portal and been sent into the quest.

Another possibility - "Tom" is one of the names that can be given to 
a ghost.  The altar on the Quest level is in a temple.  So if the
temple priest has been killed, you could end up with a ghost being 
created named Tom, and then if it either wandered onto a polymorph
trap, or got polymorphed accidentally during the polypile session,
it could have been turned into a hezrou.


